\f[B](comp)\f[R]
.PP
Equivalent to \f[CR]#\(aqidentity/1\f[R].
.SS Usage
The following examples assume \f[CR]#\(aq1+/1\f[R] is defined:
.IP
.EX
> (defun 1+ (x) (+ x 1))
1+
.EE
.IP
.EX
> (funcall (clj:comp #\(aqmath:sin/1 #\(aqmath:asin/1) 0.5)
0.49999999999999994
> (funcall (clj:comp (list #\(aq1+/1 #\(aqmath:sin/1 #\(aqmath:asin/1) 0.5))
1.5
.EE
.PP
Or used in another function call:
.IP
.EX
> (lists:filter (clj:comp #\(aqnot/1 #\(aqzero?/1)
    \(aq(0 1 0 2 0 3 0 4))
(1 2 3 4)
.EE
.PP
The usage above is best when \f[B]comp\f[R] will be called by
higher\-order functions like \f[B]lists:foldl/3\f[R] or
\f[B]lists:filter/2\f[R], etc.
However, one may also call \f[B]comp\f[R] in the following manner, best
suited for direct usage:
.IP
.EX
> (clj:comp #\(aqmath:sin/1 #\(aqmath:asin/1 0.5)
0.49999999999999994
> (clj:comp (list #\(aq1+/1 #\(aqmath:sin/1 #\(aqmath:asin/1) 0.5)
1.5
.EE
.SS Partial Application
\f[B](partial f args)\f[R]
.PP
\f[B](partial f arg\-1)\f[R]
.PP
Partially apply \f[CR]f\f[R] to a given argument \f[CR]arg\-1\f[R] or
list of \f[CR]args\f[R].
.SS Usage
.IP
.EX
> (set f (clj:partial #\(aq+/2 1))
#Fun<clj.3.121115395>
> (funcall f 2)
3
> (set f (clj:partial #\(aq+/3 1))
#Fun<clj.3.121115395>
> (funcall f \(aq(2 3))
6
> (set f (clj:partial #\(aq+/3 \(aq(2 3)))
#Fun<clj.3.121115395>
> (funcall f 4)
9
> (set f (clj:partial #\(aq+/4 \(aq(2 3)))
#Fun<clj.3.121115395>
> (funcall f \(aq(4 5))
14
.EE
.PP
Note that to partially apply a function that expects a list, you must
wrap said list into a (singleton) list.
.IP
.EX
> (set double (clj:partial #\(aq*/2 2))
#Fun<clj.5.16146786>
> (set f (clj:partial #\(aqlists:map/2 double))
#Fun<clj.5.16146786>
> (funcall f \(aq((1 2 3)))
(2 4 6)
.EE
