pred lst)\f[R]
.PP
Return \f[CR]\(aqtrue\f[R] if \f[CR](pred x)\f[R] returns
\f[CR]\(aqtrue\f[R] for any \f[CR]x\f[R] in \f[CR]lst\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](not\-any?
pred lst)\f[R]
.PP
Return \f[CR]\(aqfalse\f[R] if \f[CR](pred x)\f[R] returns
\f[CR]\(aqtrue\f[R] for any \f[CR]x\f[R] in \f[CR]lst\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](element?
elem data)\f[R]
.PP
Return \f[CR]\(aqtrue\f[R] if \f[CR]elem\f[R] is an element of
\f[CR]data\f[R], where \f[CR]data\f[R] is a list, set or ordset.
.SS Sequence Functions
\f[B](seq end)\f[R]
.PP
Equivalent to \f[CR](seq 1 end)\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](seq start end)\f[R]
.PP
Equivalent to \f[CR](seq start end 1)\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](seq start end step)\f[R]
.PP
Return a sequence of integers, starting with \f[CR]start\f[R],
containing the successive results of adding \f[CR]step\f[R] to the
previous element, until \f[CR]end\f[R] has been reached or password.
In the latter case, \f[CR]end\f[R] is not an element of the sequence.
.PP
\f[B](next func)\f[R]
.PP
Equivalent to \f[CR](next func 1 1)\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](next func start)\f[R]
.PP
Equivalent to \f[CR](next func start 1)\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](next func start step)\f[R]
.PP
Return a nullary function that returns a cons cell with \f[CR]start\f[R]
as the head and a nullary function,
\f[CR](next func (funcall func start step) step)\f[R] as the tail.
The result can be treated as a (possibly infinite) lazy list, which only
computes subsequent values as needed.
.PP
\f[B](lazy\-seq seq)\f[R]
.PP
Return a lazy sequence (possibly infinite) from given lazy sequence
\f[CR]seq\f[R] or finite lazy sequence from given list \f[CR]seq\f[R].
Lazy sequence is treated as finite if at any iteration it produces empty
list instead of data as its head and nullary function for next iteration
as its tail.
.PP
\f[B](cycle lst)\f[R]
.PP
Return a lazy infinite sequence with all elements from a given list
\f[CR]lst\f[R] or another lazy sequence cycled.
.PP
See \f[B]next/3\f[R] for details on the structure.
.PP
\f[B](range)\f[R]
.PP
Equivalent to \f[CR](range 1 1)\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](range start)\f[R]
.PP
Equivalent to \f[CR](range start 1)\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](range start step)\f[R]
.PP
Return a lazy list of integers, starting with \f[CR]start\f[R] and
increasing by \f[CR]step\f[R].
Equivalent to \f[CR](next #\(aq+/2 start step)\f[R].
See also: \f[B]next/3\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](drop n lst)\f[R]
.PP
\f[B](drop \(cqall lst)\f[R]
.PP
Return a list of all but the first \f[CR]n\f[R] elements in
\f[CR]lst\f[R].
If \f[CR]n\f[R] is the atom \f[CR]all\f[R], return the empty list.
.PP
\f[B](take n lst)\f[R]
.PP
\f[B](take \(cqall lst)\f[R]
.PP
Given a (possibly lazy) list \f[CR]lst\f[R], return a list of the first
\f[CR]n\f[R] elements of \f[CR]lst\f[R], or all elements if there are
fewer than \f[CR]n\f[R].
If \f[CR]n\f[R] is the atom \f[CR]all\f[R] and \f[CR]lst\f[R] is a
\(lqnormal\(rq list, return \f[CR]lst\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](split\-at n lst)\f[R]
.PP
Return a tuple of \f[CR]\(ga#(,(take n lst) ,(drop n lst))\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](partition n lst)\f[R]
.PP
Equivalent to \f[CR](partition n n lst)\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](partition n step lst)\f[R]
.PP
Equivalent to \f[CR](partition n step () lst)\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](partition n step pad lst)\f[R]
.PP
Return a list of lists of \f[CR]n\f[R] items each, at offsets
\f[CR]step\f[R] apart.
Use the elements of \f[CR]pad\f[R] as necessary to complete the last
partition up to \f[CR]n\f[R] elements.
In case there are not enough padding elements, return a partition with
less than \f[CR]n\f[R] items.
.PP
\f[B](partition\-all n lst)\f[R]
.PP
Equivalent to \f[CR](partition\-all n n lst)\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](partition\-all n step lst)\f[R]
.PP
Return a list of lists like \f[B]partition/3\f[R], possibly including
partitions with fewer than \f[CR]n\f[R] elements at the end.
.PP
\f[B](interleave list\-1 list\-2)\f[R]
.PP
Return a list of the first element of each list, then the second, etc.
.PP
\f[B](get\-in data keys)\f[R]
.PP
Equivalent to \f[CR](get\-in data keys \(aqundefined)\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](get\-in data keys not\-found)\f[R]
.PP
Return the value in a nested associative structure, where
\f[CR]keys\f[R] is a list of keys or list indices.
Return the atom \f[CR]not\-found\f[R] if the key is not present or index
is out of bounds, or the \f[CR]not\-found\f[R] value.
.PP
\f[B](reduce func (cons head tail))\f[R]
.PP
Equivalent to \f[CR](reduce func head tail)\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](reduce func acc lst)\f[R]
.PP
Equivalent to \f[CR](lists:foldl func acc lst)\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](repeat x)\f[R]
.PP
Return a lazy infinite sequence of \f[CR]x\f[R]s.
.PP
See \f[B]next/3\f[R] for details on the structure.
.PP
\f[B](repeat n f)\f[R]
.PP
Given a nullary function \f[CR]f\f[R], return a list of \f[CR]n\f[R]
applications of \f[CR]f\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](repeat n x)\f[R]
.PP
Given a term \f[CR]x\f[R], return a list of \f[CR]n\f[R] copies of
\f[CR]x\f[R].
.SS Other Functions
\f[B](identity x)\f[R]
.PP
Identity function.
.PP
\f[B](constantly x)\f[R]
.PP
Return a unary function that returns \f[CR]x\f[R].
N.B.
This is like Haskell\(cqs \f[CR]const\f[R] rather than Clojure\(cqs
\f[CR]constantly\f[R].
.PP
\f[B](inc x)\f[R]
.PP
Increment \f[CR]x\f[R] by 1.
.PP
\f[B](dec x)\f[R]
.PP
Decrement \f[CR]x\f[R] by 1.
